---
/**
 * Terms of use.
 *
 * Owner: trust-compliance-officer (docs/agents/ROSTER.md §3). Rewritten for this session, not
 * appended to: the previous revision was an interim baseline written before the policy set existed.
 *
 * Two disciplines govern this file. First, it states only what the product provably does — verified
 * at the reviewed date: packages/rights-engine exports nothing and no rule data exists, so no
 * rights output is produced anywhere; FLIGHT_PROVIDER is `fixture` and no provider licence is
 * recorded in docs/PROVIDER_LICENSING.md, so live flight data is Unavailable; there is no account,
 * no purchase, and no code path that submits anything to a third party on a user's behalf.
 *
 * Second, the quoted disclaimers are verbatim fixed text — the independence disclaimer from
 * DIRECTIVE.md §3.4 and three of the §26 result disclaimers. They are rendered from constants so
 * that reformatting cannot reflow them, and they are not paraphrasable: AGENTS.md §7 requires
 * escalation rather than an edit if one reads badly.
 *
 * The liability and governing-law section is deliberately unwritten. Naming a jurisdiction the
 * operator has not designated would be an invented fact (AGENTS.md §1.1), and a limitation of
 * liability that no lawyer has read is worth less than the honesty of saying so.
 */
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro'
import { disclaimers } from '../lib/copy/disclaimers.ts'
import { results } from '../lib/copy/results.ts'

const title = 'Terms of use — DelayPilot'
const description =
  'DelayPilot is an informational tool. It never files a claim, never books or purchases anything, and never represents you. The terms, stated plainly.'

/**
 * The quoted text below is imported, never retyped.
 *
 * apps/web/src/lib/copy/disclaimers.ts and results.ts (owner: ux-copy-steward) hold the fixed
 * strings transcribed byte for byte from DIRECTIVE.md, and their test re-reads DIRECTIVE.md and
 * fails on a single changed character. Declaring a second copy of a verbatim disclaimer in this
 * file would create exactly the drift that test exists to prevent.
 */
const INDEPENDENCE_DISCLAIMER = disclaimers.independence
const FLIGHT_DATA_DISCLAIMER = disclaimers.flightData
const PREDICTION_DISCLAIMER = disclaimers.prediction
const RIGHTS_DISCLAIMER = disclaimers.rights
const DEMO_LABEL = results.demo
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={description}>
  <article class="prose">
    <h1>Terms of use</h1>

    <p>
      <strong>DelayPilot is an information tool, and only that.</strong> It never files a claim, a complaint,
      or a refund request. It never books, purchases, rebooks, or cancels anything. It never represents
      you, and it never signs in anywhere as you. What it does is tell you what is known about a flight,
      how much to trust that information, and which published rules may apply to your situation — with
      the source, the version, and the timestamp attached.
    </p>

    <p class="meta">Reviewed <time datetime="2026-09-12">12 September 2026</time>.</p>

    <h2>What DelayPilot is</h2>

    <blockquote>
      <p>{INDEPENDENCE_DISCLAIMER}</p>
    </blockquote>

    <p>
      Airline, airport, and regulator names, and IATA or ICAO codes, appear on this site only to
      identify a flight, a place, or a rule. Their use does not imply any relationship. DelayPilot
      is not affiliated with, endorsed by, appointed by, or acting for any airline, airport,
      government agency, or regulator, and it does not display any third party's logo, wordmark, or
      brand styling.
    </p>

    <h2>What DelayPilot never does on your behalf</h2>

    <ul>
      <li>
        It never submits a claim, complaint, refund request, rebooking, or purchase for you — not to
        an airline, not to a regulator, not to anyone.
      </li>
      <li>It never acts as your agent, your representative, or your legal adviser.</li>
      <li>
        It never reads your email inbox, scrapes an airline account, or authenticates as you to a
        third party.
      </li>
      <li>
        It never asks for a booking reference or record locator, a passport, a government ID, a
        Known Traveler Number, a redress number, a card number, or an airline account password.
      </li>
      <li>
        It never classifies an emergency and never gives safety-critical aviation advice. Weather
        and airspace information describes operating conditions, nothing more.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <p>
      These are not preferences that a future release might relax. They are invariants the codebase
      is built and reviewed against, and a change to any of them would be a change to what this
      product is.
    </p>

    <h2>Passenger-rights information is an estimate, not a decision</h2>

    <p>
      Where DelayPilot describes passenger rights, it describes what published rules{' '}
      <strong>may</strong> mean for the facts you entered. Every assessment is tied to a named, dated
      rule-set version with links to the official source, and is limited to five outcomes: a rule likely
      applies, may apply, is not indicated, cannot be determined from what is known, or is a future rule
      that is not yet in force. DelayPilot never tells you that a payment is due to you, never states
      that an airline is required to pay you, and never presents an assessment as a determination. Only
      the airline or the relevant regulator can decide your case.
    </p>

    <blockquote>
      <p>{RIGHTS_DISCLAIMER}</p>
    </blockquote>

    <p>
      <strong>Today, no rule set is in force.</strong> The rights engine carries no rule data, so this
      site produces no rights assessment at all. Any passenger-rights writing you find here is general
      explanation with its official source linked, and the only rights output that will ever appear before
      a rule set is published is inside clearly labelled demonstration content.
    </p>

    <h2>Flight information changes, and can be wrong</h2>

    <p>
      Operational flight information comes from third-party data providers and from official
      aviation sources. It can be incomplete, delayed, or wrong, and it changes while you are
      reading it. Every value DelayPilot displays carries a label saying where it came from and how
      fresh it is, and where no trustworthy answer exists the interface says so rather than
      estimating one.
    </p>

    <blockquote>
      <p>{FLIGHT_DATA_DISCLAIMER}</p>
    </blockquote>

    <p>
      Today no flight-data provider is connected and no provider licence is recorded, so live flight
      data is <strong>Unavailable</strong> and the site says exactly that.
    </p>

    <h2>Risk and connection assessments are estimates</h2>

    <p>
      Delay risk, cancellation risk, and connection assessments are estimates derived from the
      information available at the time. DelayPilot shows a qualitative risk band rather than a
      percentage unless a calibrated model has been validated and deployed, because a number implies
      a precision that an uncalibrated model does not have.
    </p>

    <blockquote>
      <p>{PREDICTION_DISCLAIMER}</p>
    </blockquote>

    <h2>Demonstration content is fiction</h2>

    <p>
      To show what the product does without paid data, DelayPilot uses a deterministic, invented
      itinerary. Every panel built from it carries this label:
    </p>

    <blockquote>
      <p>{DEMO_LABEL}</p>
    </blockquote>

    <p>
      Demonstration content never uses a real flight number with invented live details, and is never
      indexed as though it were a real flight.
    </p>

    <h2>No warranty, and no guarantee of availability</h2>

    <p>
      This site is provided as it is, without warranty of accuracy, completeness, or fitness for a
      particular purpose. It may be changed, interrupted, or withdrawn at any time. Do not rely on
      it alone for a decision that cannot be reversed: confirm critical details with the operating
      airline and the airport before you act.
    </p>

    <h2>Using this site</h2>

    <p>
      The site is free to read and needs no account. You may read, print, and share its pages. The
      text, design, and original explanation remain DelayPilot's; regulatory text and official
      source material belong to the bodies that publish it, and is linked rather than reproduced
      wholesale. Do not use this site in a way that breaks the law or that interferes with its
      operation for other readers.
    </p>

    <h2>Liability and governing law</h2>

    <p>
      <strong>This section is deliberately incomplete.</strong> The operator has not yet designated a
      governing jurisdiction, and no limitation of liability has been through legal review. Rather than
      copy a clause from elsewhere and present it as agreed, DelayPilot states the position plainly: no
      such clause is asserted here, and nothing on this page is intended to remove a consumer protection
      that the law of your country gives you. This section will be written once a jurisdiction is designated
      and a lawyer has reviewed the result.
    </p>

    <h2>Changes to these terms</h2>

    <p>
      These terms are rewritten rather than added to when the product changes — an account system, a
      purchase, a saved trip, or a published rule set each change what there is to govern. The
      reviewed date at the top of this page records when its description last matched the site.
    </p>

    <h2>Related policies</h2>

    <ul>
      <li><a href="/privacy/">Privacy</a> — what is collected today, and what never will be.</li>
      <li>
        <a href="/editorial-policy/">Editorial policy</a> — how guidance is sourced, reviewed, and corrected.
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href="/advertising-policy/">Advertising policy</a> and{' '}
        <a href="/affiliate-disclosure/">affiliate disclosure</a> — how DelayPilot does and does not make
        money.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
